[38;2;124;124;124mLet me think about this. X = G/K is a noncompact symmetric space of dimension n.[0m[38;2;124;124;124mSmall geodesic balls in X grow as r^n (standard Euclidean behavior at small [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mscales, since any Riemannian manifold is locally Euclidean).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mM = Γ\G/K is a locally symmetric space, where Γ is discrete and torsion-free. [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mThe quotient map π: X → M is a local isometry (covering map, since Γ is torsion-[0m[38;2;124;124;124mfree and discrete, acting freely).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe question: does |B_M(x̃, r)| ~ r^n for every x̃ ∈ M and sufficiently small r?[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFor a Riemannian manifold, small geodesic balls always grow as r^n where n is [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mthe dimension — this is just the local Euclidean behavior. The volume of a small[0m[38;2;124;124;124mgeodesic ball in any n-dimensional Riemannian manifold is (ω_n) r^n (1 + O(r^2))[0m[38;2;124;124;124mwhere ω_n is the volume of the unit Euclidean ball.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mBut there's a subtlety: "for every x̃ ∈ M and sufficiently small r". The issue is[0m[38;2;124;124;124mwhether "sufficiently small" can be chosen uniformly, or whether it depends on [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mthe point. Also, the question is whether this holds at every point.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mActually, for any smooth Riemannian manifold of dimension n, at every point the [0m[38;2;124;124;124mvolume of a small geodesic ball grows as r^n. This is a local statement and [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mholds at every point. The injectivity radius may vary, but at each point there [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mexists some r_0(x̃) > 0 such that for r < r_0(x̃), the geodesic ball is [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mdiffeomorphic to a Euclidean ball and the volume is ω_n r^n (1 + O(r^2)).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo the answer is yes. The key points:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m1. M = Γ\G/K is a smooth Riemannian manifold of dimension n (since Γ is discrete[0m[38;2;124;124;124mand torsion-free, the action is free and properly discontinuous, so the quotient[0m[38;2;124;124;124mis a manifold).[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m2. The projection π: X → M is a local isometry.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m3. For any point x̃ ∈ M, choose a lift x ∈ X. For r smaller than the injectivity [0m[38;2;124;124;124mradius at x̃ (and also small enough that the exponential map is a [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mdiffeomorphism), the geodesic ball B_M(x̃, r) is isometric to B_X(x, r) via the [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mlocal inverse of π.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m4. Since X is a Riemannian manifold of dimension n, |B_X(x, r)| ~ ω_n r^n as r →[0m[38;2;124;124;124m0.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m5. Therefore |B_M(x̃, r)| ~ ω_n r^n ~ r^n.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe torsion-free condition ensures Γ acts freely, so M is a genuine manifold (no[0m[38;2;124;124;124morbifold singularities). This is important — if Γ had torsion, there would be [0m
[38;2;124;124;124morbifold points where the local structure is a cone, and the volume growth would[0m[38;2;124;124;124mdiffer at those points.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo the answer is YES, and the torsion-free condition is essential.[0m

